1. Why we created domain first
a. Clean Architecture Principle: Dependency Rule
┌─────────────────────────────────┐
│         API Layer               │  ← Depends on ↓
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│    Infrastructure Layer         │  ← Depends on ↓
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│    Application Layer            │  ← Depends on ↓
├─────────────────────────────────┤
│    Domain Layer (CORE)          │  ← Depends on NOTHING
└─────────────────────────────────┘

The Rule: Dependencies point INWARD only. Domain has ZERO dependencies.

Why this matters:

Domain = Pure business logic
Domain doesn't know about databases, APIs, frameworks
Domain can't accidentally depend on infrastructure
Domain is the most stable layer (changes least)

b. Inside-Out Development (Domain-Driven)
Traditional Approach (Outside-In):
Database → Repository → Service → Controller → Business Logic
❌ Problem: Business logic becomes scattered

Clean Architecture (Inside-Out):
Domain → Application → Infrastructure → API
✅ Benefit: Business logic is centralized and protected

Core stability - Most important code (domain) is written first
No framework coupling - Domain doesn't depend on EF Core, ASP.NET
Testable - Domain logic can be tested without databases/APIs
Flexible - Can swap databases, frameworks without touching domain

I start with the Domain layer because it represents the core business logic and should have zero dependencies 
on frameworks or infrastructure. This follows the Dependency Inversion Principle from Clean Architecture.

By starting domain-first:
1. Business logic is encapsulated in entities (rich domain model)
2. Domain can be tested without databases or APIs
3. Domain models aren't dictated by database constraints
4. We can change infrastructure (swap PostgreSQL for MongoDB) without touching domain

This is the 'inside-out' approach - build the most important, stable layer first, then build infrastructure to support it. 
The opposite (database-first) often leads to anemic domain models where entities are just data bags and business logic is scattered.


